 ## Approach
 
-Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. The approach for delivering committed changes is still open: five-second HTTPS polling, self-hosted WebSockets, or a hosted realtime service. All three can meet the required freshness target. Subsecond updates would be useful, but are not required.
+Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. Committed changes reach browsers over self-hosted WebSockets: the server holds a session per connected browser and pushes each committed event revision to the sessions watching that event. All connected browsers show a committed change within 15 seconds; subsecond delivery is useful but not required.
 
-Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery must be designed together after this choice. Polling would favor cursor queries; self-hosted sockets would need connection management and replay; hosted delivery would need publication reconciliation and provider credentials. Neither operating cost nor minimum operational burden has been declared the overriding criterion.
+Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery are designed together. The change store keeps replayable revisions so a reconnecting browser can catch up from the last revision it displayed, and publication must be reliable so a committed change is not missed. Delivery owns socket capacity, session lifecycle, and publication; browsers own reconnect and catch-up. The project accepts the connection and reconnect operations work this requires rather than a recurring realtime-vendor bill and provider coupling.
 
